# SYNOPSIS

```usage
readlink [-fn] path
```

# DESCRIPTION

`readlink` prints the target of the symbolic link <path>, as it is stored, then a newline.

: `-f`
  Print instead the absolute path <path> leads to, every symbolic link on the way followed and every `.` and `..` resolved, as C's `realpath` makes it; <path> need not be a link, but it and each directory on the way must exist.
: `-n`
  Print no newline.

`readlink` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/readlink`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/readlink`.
There is no native `readlink`.
Symbolic links are made and read with the posix extension of 9P (see symlink(5)), which tmpfs(4) and fsd(4) serve.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace, in `/tmp`:

```text
% ln -s /tmp/sb/f /tmp/sb/link
% readlink /tmp/sb/link
/tmp/sb/f
```

# SEE ALSO

ln(1), symlink(5), namespace(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/readlink.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

When <path> is not a symbolic link or cannot be found, `readlink` reports `readlink: readlink` <path>`:` and the error on standard error, or `readlink: realpath` <path>`:` with `-f`, and exits 1.
A <path> of 4096 bytes or more is reported as `path too long`; no <path>, more than one or an unknown flag prints the usage message; each exits 1.
Otherwise `readlink` exits 0, or 1 if the output could not be written.

# BUGS

`-f` is not POSIX's; POSIX's `readlink` has `-n` alone.

`-f` is untested on VectraOS.
